---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const animalCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The nutrition conversion', note: 'Feed conversion ratio (kg feed per kg gain): chicken ~1.7, pork ~3, beef ~6–10 — the trophic lesson\'s ten-percent logic, bred and fed to its efficient edge. The ratio drives both price and carbon footprint.' },
    { math: '2 · Breeding programmes', note: 'Selective breeding transformed growth rates (broilers reach market weight in 6 weeks vs 12 in 1950 — welfare debates included); artificial insemination and genomic selection compressed generations. The genetics lesson\'s heritability arithmetic, industrialised.' },
    { math: '3 · Health = economics', note: 'Biosecurity (quarantine, disinfection) prevents what antibiotics used to cure; vaccination schedules; welfare indicators (lameness, behaviour) correlate with productivity — the welfare-science convergence is measurable, not sentimental.' },
    { math: '4 · The systems spectrum', note: 'Pasture (low input, low density), mixed crop-livestock (nutrient cycling — the pasture feeds the soil), intensive (controlled environment, highest efficiency per animal, highest capital). Each fits a different land, labour and capital situation (the economics lesson\'s factor endowments, farmed).' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/agriculture/intermediate/animal-husbandry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Animal husbandry is <strong>applied physiology, genetics and economics wearing hooves</strong>:
    conversion ratios decide viability, breeding programmes compound gains, and welfare has
    become a measured production variable rather than an afterthought.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="conversion, breeding, health, the systems spectrum" widget="equationStep" config={animalCfg} />

  <h2>The stakes and the debates</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Antibiotic resistance is agriculture\'s shared bill:</strong> ~70% of the world\'s antibiotics go to animals (growth promotion now banned in the EU and increasingly elsewhere) — the infection lesson\'s resistance logic crosses species lines; stewardship is One Health.</li>
    <li><strong>Livestock\'s climate ledger:</strong> ~14.5% of global emissions (methane — short-lived but potent; the mitigation lesson\'s fast lever) — solutions run from feed additives (3-NOP cuts enteric methane ~30%) to systems change; the honest accounting is per-kg-product and per-hectare, both.</li>
    <li><strong>Welfare science standardised:</strong> the Five Freedoms framework (from fear, hunger, pain…) and now positive-welfare indicators — cage-free transitions, controlled-atmosphere stunning: consumer pressure plus measurement moving an industry.</li>
    <li><strong>Aquaculture arrived quietly huge:</strong> over half of seafood eaten is farmed now — the fastest-growing protein sector, with its own conversion-ratio and disease lessons (salmon feed ratios fell ~2× in two decades).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> FAO Livestock\'s Long Shadow revisions; the Five Freedoms (Farm Animal Welfare Council); the trophic and genetics lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
